This is a strong DevOps/SRE interview project because it combines backup automation, AWS, security, DR, Linux scripting, S3 CRR, IAM, Secrets Manager, and configuration management with Ansible.
Below is a version you can use to prepare and speak about the project in an interview. I’ve structured it so you can first give a 1–2 minute overview, then go deeper if the interviewer asks.

1. Project Introduction — 1–2 Minute Answer

“I worked on implementing an automated configuration backup and disaster recovery solution for GitLab and Nexus in the production environment.

The main objective was to make sure critical configuration files were securely backed up and available in a separate AWS region in case of a production failure or regional outage.

We automated the process using shell scripts running on EC2. The scripts collect the required GitLab and Nexus configuration files, package them into a compressed .tar.gz archive, encrypt the archive using OpenSSL AES-256-CBC with PBKDF2, and upload the encrypted backup to an S3 bucket in Frankfurt.

We configured S3 Cross-Region Replication, so the encrypted backup is automatically replicated from Frankfurt to Paris, which acts as the DR region.

For security, the encryption password is not hardcoded in the script. It is stored in AWS Secrets Manager and retrieved at runtime using the EC2 IAM role. The EC2 instance has only the required permissions to access S3 and Secrets Manager.

We also implemented a checksum-based change detection mechanism for GitLab. Before creating a backup, the script calculates a SHA-256 checksum of the relevant configuration files and compares it with the previous checksum. If there are no changes, the backup is skipped. This avoids unnecessary backup creation and S3 uploads.

Finally, we created a restoration script that downloads the encrypted backup from S3, retrieves the secret from Secrets Manager, decrypts the archive, and extracts the configuration files.

In addition to the backup solution, we worked on configuration management using Git, GitLab CI/CD, and Ansible so that GitLab configuration changes are version-controlled, reviewed through merge requests, and automatically deployed to the appropriate GitLab nodes.”**

That is a very good opening answer. Then you can break the architecture down.

