12. Explain the Checksum Mechanism
This is one of the more interesting parts of your implementation.

Your script generates:

CURRENT_SUM=$(tar -cf - \
  /etc/gitlab/gitlab.rb \
  /etc/gitlab/gitlab-secrets.json \
  /etc/gitlab/ssl \
  /etc/gitlab/trusted-certs \
  /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key* \
  /etc/fstab \
  2>/dev/null | sha256sum | awk '{print $1}')

Then compares it with:

last-backup.checksum

Interview explanation:
“We wanted to avoid creating and uploading a backup every time the cron job executes. So before creating the archive, we generate a SHA-256 checksum based on the relevant configuration content.

We compare that checksum with the checksum generated during the previous successful backup.

If they are identical, we assume there were no relevant configuration changes and skip the backup.

If the checksum differs, we create a new backup, upload it to S3, and update the stored checksum.”

Flow:

Cron
 ↓
Calculate SHA-256
 ↓
Compare with previous checksum
 ↓
 ┌───────────────┐
 │               │
Same           Different
 │               │
Exit             ↓
             Create Backup
                 ↓
             Upload S3
                 ↓
          Save New Checksum

