39. Your End-to-End Interview Story
If the interviewer says:

“Tell me about a DevOps automation project you worked on.”

You can tell this story:

“One project I worked on was automating disaster recovery for GitLab and Nexus configuration.

Previously, configuration backup and recovery involved more manual effort. We designed an automated process where a scheduled job runs on the production EC2 host and collects the required configuration files.

For GitLab, this included gitlab.rb, gitlab-secrets.json, SSL certificates, trusted certificates, SSH host keys, and filesystem configuration. For Nexus, we backed up the Helm and Kubernetes configuration required to redeploy the environment.

The script first performs a SHA-256 checksum comparison to determine whether the configuration has changed. If there are no changes, it skips the backup.

If changes are detected, it packages the files into a .tar.gz archive and retrieves the encryption password dynamically from AWS Secrets Manager using the EC2 IAM role. The archive is encrypted using OpenSSL AES-256-CBC with PBKDF2 and then uploaded to an S3 bucket in Frankfurt.

S3 Cross-Region Replication automatically replicates the encrypted backup to our Paris DR region.

We also implemented a restoration script. During recovery, the encrypted file is downloaded from S3, the secret is retrieved from Secrets Manager, the archive is decrypted, and the configuration is extracted for restoration.

In parallel, we worked on managing GitLab configuration through Git and Ansible. Configuration changes are made through feature branches, reviewed through merge requests, and deployed automatically through GitLab CI/CD. Ansible uses Jinja2 templates and node-specific variables to generate the appropriate configuration and runs gitlab-ctl reconfigure when changes are detected.

Overall, the solution improved automation, security, consistency, and disaster recovery readiness for our GitLab and Nexus environments.”

